# The Luvo web app.
#
# Copy to `.env.local`. Everything here is NEXT_PUBLIC_* and therefore inlined
# into the browser bundle at build time — nothing secret may be added to this
# file. Clerk's secret key and Stripe's live key belong in the deployment's own
# environment and are read only inside a route handler.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Luvo API. Everything real comes from here: the catalogue, the renders, the
# generation queue, the credit ledger.
#
# Locally, run the whole backend in memory from the repo root — `npm run sandbox`
# — which serves on :8099 with no Railway, no R2 and no fal, and therefore no way
# to spend money. See docs/sandbox.md.
#
# With this unset the site still builds and every page still renders, but there
# is no catalogue and nothing can be generated. The footer says so rather than
# showing an empty grid with no explanation.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8099

# This site's own origin. Used for canonical links, Open Graph tags, the sitemap
# and the share fallback url. No trailing slash.
#
# On Vercel this falls back to the deployment's own URL, so a preview branch does
# not advertise production's canonical.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Clerk, as a SECOND sign-in provider. Sign-in itself works without it: it is a
# six-digit code from the API, so `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` above is the whole of its
# configuration, and whether a code can actually be mailed is a server setting
# (`RESEND_API_KEY`, or `EMAIL_DEV_ECHO` locally). Clerk is worth having because
# a Google button is one tap where a code is a trip to an inbox; it lands as
# another token handed to `POST /v1/account/sign-in`, which the server already
# verifies. `CLERK_ISSUER` is the API's side of it.
#
# With this unset, `<SignInDialog>` falls back to the mailed code and
# `<ClerkProvider>` is not rendered at all — it throws with no key, so a fresh
# clone and the sandbox both depend on that fallback.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no Stripe key here, and that is not an omission.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Checkout is HOSTED: the visitor leaves for Stripe's own page and comes back, so
# this app has no card field, no Stripe.js and nothing for a publishable key to
# configure. Both Stripe credentials are secrets on the API —
# `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` and `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`, plus `CHECKOUT_RETURN_URL`
# pointing back at this site. See server/.env.example.
#
# Whether checkout is open is reported by `GET /v1/credits` as `checkout:
# true|false`, so a build cannot disagree with the server about whether anybody
# can be charged. `NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` used to be read here to
# decide whether to draw a Buy button; it was a guess about a setting on another
# process, and it is gone.

# Google Search Console's html-tag verification token, without the meta wrapper —
# just the `content` value. A DNS record is the better proof and does not depend
# on a deploy; this exists so the token has somewhere to live other than pasted
# into a component. Unset emits no tag, which is the ordinary state.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=
